 the post-hoc analyses assessing the impact of preoperative pain prevalence, 16 procedures were labeled as &#x0201c;high prevalence&#x0201d; and nine as &#x0201c;low prevalence&#x0201d;. When preoperative pain prevalence was added to the model, the odds ratio for high preoperative pain prevalence was 2.56 (1.75 to 3.73, P &#x0003c; 0.001) and the overall odds ratio of preoperative pain level decreased from 4.45 to 3.37 (2.52 to 4.51, P &#x0003c; 0.001). </p><table-wrap id="A81366TBL3" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Table 3.</label><caption><title>Preoperative Pain as Predictor of Moderate and Severe Acute Postoperative Pain<sup><xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="A81366TBL3FN1">a</xref>,<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="A81366TBL3FN2">b</xref></sup></title></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th style="text-align: left;" rowspan="2" colspan="1">Procedures</th><th rowspan="2" colspan="1">N<sup><xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="A81366TBL3FN3">c</xref></sup></th><th rowspan="2" colspan="1">Odds Ratio</th><th style="border-bottom: solid thin;" colspan="2" rowspan="1">CI (95%)</th><th rowspan="2" colspan="1">P Value</th></tr><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lower</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Upper</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
